```

This preserves the user's context throughout the entire interaction.

The product should not introduce a separate navigation destination for application history.

### Core principle

> **The user moves deeper into the same Timeline surface rather than navigating through additional screens.**

---

# 12. Screenshots

Screenshots are an **optional visual layer**.

They are not the foundation of Timeline.

Their purpose is to give the user visual evidence of what they were doing, turning an otherwise abstract activity record into something more tangible.

Instead of:

> YouTube — 38 minutes

the user may see:

> YouTube — 38 minutes
> `[visual snapshot]`

The core product remains useful without screenshots.

### Android

Android may support screenshot capture through the appropriate screen-capture mechanisms and explicit user permission.

The implementation must respect platform permissions, indicators, background execution requirements, and system restrictions.

### iOS

iOS does not provide the same unrestricted background screen-capture model.

Timeline therefore treats screenshots as a **platform-enhanced capability**, not a cross-platform requirement.

---

# 13. Floating Overlay

Android may optionally provide a small floating Timeline element while the user is using another application.

The purpose is not to create a permanent dashboard.

It should remain subtle and potentially expose only minimal information such as:

- Current session duration
- A lightweight Timeline control
- A small indication that tracking is active

This is an optional Android-specific enhancement and requires the appropriate overlay permission.

---

# 14. Daily Summary

Timeline may expose a compact summary of the current day without introducing a dedicated dashboard.

For example:

```text
TODAY

5h 42m
total phone usage

47 sessions

Most used
YouTube · 1h 42m

23 apps used
```

The summary can appear as part of the Timeline surface.

It is subordinate to the chronological timeline itself.

The product should never become primarily a statistics dashboard.

---

# 15. Two-Screen Architecture

Timeline has exactly **two navigable screens**.

## Screen 1 — Timeline

The primary product experience.

It contains or can reveal:

- Chronological activity
- Application sessions
- Date selection
- Time-of-day selection
- Optional screenshots
- Daily summary
- Session Detail bottom sheet
- Expanded Application Timeline
- Usage information

All of these belong to the Timeline experience.

They are not additional navigation destinations.

## Screen 2 — Settings

Settings contains configuration and system-related controls, including:

- Usage tracking
- Screenshot capture
- Floating overlay
- Permissions
- Privacy
- Data retention
- Tracking preferences

### No additional destinations

Timeline should not introduce:

```text
Timeline | Apps | Overview | Profile
```

or:

```text
Home | Stats | Apps | Settings
```

There is no dedicated:

- Apps screen
- Overview screen
- Profile screen
- Session screen
- Application screen
- Calendar screen

The Timeline remains the central product surface.

