# 18. Core Data Model

The fundamental Timeline object is an activity event.

```text
Activity Event

Application
Start time
End time
Duration
Previous application
Next application
Screenshot
Date
Session ID
```

From this structure, Timeline can construct:

```text
Activity Events
       ↓
Session History
       ↓
Daily Timeline
       ↓
Application Timeline
       ↓
Derived Statistics
       ↓
Optional Intelligence
```

The application timeline is therefore derived from the same underlying session/event model rather than requiring a separate data concept.

---

# 19. Tracking Philosophy

Timeline should be **event-driven rather than continuously active**.

The system should perform the minimum work necessary to maintain an accurate activity record.

Conceptually:

```text
No transition
      ↓
Lightweight state
      ↓
Application transition detected
      ↓
Record event
      ↓
Create or update session
      ↓
Return to lightweight state
```

The product should avoid unnecessary continuous processing.

This is important both for battery consumption and for the trustworthiness of the product.

Timeline should never become a significant source of battery drain simply because it is observing phone activity.

---

# 20. Android Service Architecture

On Android, persistent tracking should be owned by the tracking service rather than by the launcher Activity.

The Activity is responsible for entering the product and initiating the tracking experience when necessary.

The tracking service owns:

- Session tracking
- Activity transitions
- Notifications
- Persistent tracking state
- Background execution
- Relevant system integrations

The Activity should not be tightly coupled to the lifetime of the tracking service.

Conceptually:

```text
Launcher Activity
       │
       │ starts tracking
       ▼
Tracking Service
       │
       ├── Activity/session tracking
       ├── Notifications
       ├── Persistent state
       └── Optional screenshot/overlay systems
```

Clearing the application from Recents should not inherently terminate tracking unless that behavior is explicitly intended.

Android OEMs may impose their own background-management policies, so Timeline should design for standard Android lifecycle behavior while acknowledging that some manufacturers may terminate background services more aggressively.

---

# 21. Battery and Resource Philosophy

Timeline must be designed around resource restraint.

The application should:

- Avoid unnecessary continuous processing.
- Record transitions rather than repeatedly polling when platform APIs provide event-driven alternatives.
- Keep background work minimal.
- Avoid unnecessary screenshot activity.
- Avoid excessive database writes.
- Avoid unnecessary network activity.
- Keep notifications and overlays lightweight.

The principle is:

> **Timeline should observe the phone without becoming another demanding application running on it.**

---

# 22. Platform Strategy

Timeline is a cross-platform product, but Android and iOS do not need identical technical capabilities.

## Shared

The shared product layer should contain:

- Activity/session models
- Timeline models
- Aggregation logic
- Statistics calculations
- Local data structures
- Business rules
- Shared UI concepts
- Presentation logic

## Android-specific

Android can provide platform-specific capabilities such as:

- Usage access
- Foreground application detection
- Foreground tracking service
- Screen capture
- Floating overlay
- Android-specific background mechanisms

## iOS-specific

iOS should use the activity and usage APIs available within Apple's platform restrictions.

The product principle is:

> **Same product concept, platform-appropriate capabilities.**

The iOS implementation should not force Android-specific functionality into the shared product model.

---

# 23. Privacy Model

Timeline deals with inherently personal activity data.

The product should therefore treat user activity as private by default.

The core experience should not require sending activity data to a remote service.

Where possible, activity data should remain under the user's control on the device.

Privacy-related controls belong in Settings and should make it clear:

- What Timeline records
- Whether screenshots are enabled
- What permissions are active
- How long activity data is retained
- How tracking can be disabled
- How stored activity can be removed

The product should never make the user feel that their activity is being collected for someone else.

Timeline exists primarily so **the user can understand their own activity**.

---

# 24. What Timeline Does Not Do

These boundaries are part of the product definition.

## No AI in the Core Product

Timeline does not need to interpret behavior.

It records:

> YouTube — 38 minutes.

It does not automatically conclude:

> You appear to be procrastinating.

The user decides what their activity means.

## No Productivity Score

There is no:

> 73% productive day.

## No Gamification

There are no achievement systems or streak mechanics designed to manipulate engagement.

## No Judgment

Timeline does not tell users:

> You spent too much time on social media.

## No Forced Categorization

Timeline does not need to decide whether an application is:

- Productive
- Entertainment
- Education
- Social
- Distracting

The user's behavior is presented without imposing a value judgment.

---

# 25. Future Intelligence

Intelligence may eventually be added, but it must sit **above the mechanical Timeline system**.

The architecture should remain:

```text
               Timeline Events
                      │
                      ▼
              Session History
                      │
                      ▼
               Visual Timeline
                      │
          ┌───────────┴───────────┐
          ▼                       ▼
       User view             Intelligence
```

Future intelligence could answer questions such as:

- What did I spend most of my evening doing?
- How many times did I switch away from Android Studio?
- What usually happens before I open YouTube?
- Show me my biggest distractions this week.

But intelligence is an enhancement.

The underlying Timeline must remain useful without it.

